(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-endocrine-quiz"]={"title":"Endocrine System — Anatomy Exam 3","category":"Anatomy","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"According to the lecture, which structure functions as an endocrine organ, contains autonomic nervous system centers, AND exerts control over the suprarenal medulla?","choices":["Hypothalamus","Pituitary gland","Pineal gland","Thymus gland"],"answer":0,"exp":"The hypothalamus fills all three roles: it acts as an endocrine organ (releasing ADH and oxytocin toward the pituitary), it contains autonomic nervous system centers, and it controls the suprarenal medulla. — Overview — 'The Hypothalamus and Endocrine Regulation'"},{"q":"The lecture lists several 'other endocrine tissues' beyond the main organs. Which of the following is named among them?","choices":["Spleen","Kidney","Liver","Gallbladder"],"answer":1,"exp":"The kidney is listed among other endocrine tissues (it produces erythropoietin and calcitriol per Figure 19.1). — Overview — 'Other endocrine tissues'; Figure 19.1"},{"q":"In Figure 19.1, which hormone is identified as the secretion of the pineal gland?","choices":["Calcitonin","Thymosins","Melatonin","Erythropoietin"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pineal gland is labeled with melatonin in Figure 19.1. — Figure 19.1 — The Endocrine System"},{"q":"Per Figure 19.1, the heart is shown as an endocrine tissue producing which of the following?","choices":["Leptin and resistin","Thyroxine and triiodothyronine","Insulin and glucagon","Atrial and brain natriuretic peptides"],"answer":3,"exp":"The heart is labeled as producing natriuretic peptides — atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) and brain natriuretic peptide (BNP). — Figure 19.1 — Heart"},{"q":"According to Figure 19.1, adipose tissue is depicted as secreting which pair of hormones?","choices":["Leptin and resistin","Cortisol and aldosterone","Estrogens and progestins","EPO and calcitriol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Adipose tissue is labeled with leptin and resistin. — Figure 19.1 — Adipose Tissue"},{"q":"In Figure 19.2, the hypothalamus controls the suprarenal medulla by which of the three listed mechanisms?","choices":["Secreting regulatory hormones to the pars distalis","Controlling sympathetic output via preganglionic motor fibers","Producing ADH and oxytocin","Releasing melatonin into the portal system"],"answer":1,"exp":"Figure 19.2 shows mechanism 3: control of sympathetic output to the suprarenal medullae via preganglionic motor fibers, driving secretion of epinephrine and norepinephrine. — Figure 19.2 — Hypothalamic Control over Endocrine Organs"},{"q":"The pituitary gland sits within which bony fossa?","choices":["Jugular fossa","Pterygopalatine fossa","Hypophyseal fossa of the sella turcica","Mandibular fossa"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pituitary (hypophysis) sits in the hypophyseal fossa of the sella turcica of the sphenoid bone. — 'The Pituitary Gland' — location; 'Pituitary Gland (Hypophysis Cerebri)'"},{"q":"The pituitary gland is attached to the hypothalamus by which structure?","choices":["Diaphragma sellae","Dorsum sellae","Optic chiasma","Infundibulum"],"answer":3,"exp":"The infundibulum (pituitary stalk) attaches the pituitary to the hypothalamus, connecting the pars nervosa to the median eminence. — 'The Pituitary Gland'; Figure 19.3a"},{"q":"The diaphragma sellae covering the pituitary is pierced centrally by the infundibulum and separates the gland from which structure?","choices":["Optic chiasma","Cavernous sinus","Sphenoid air sinus","Dorsum sellae"],"answer":0,"exp":"The diaphragma sellae of dura mater is pierced centrally by the infundibulum and separates the gland from the optic chiasma. — 'Pituitary Gland (Hypophysis Cerebri)' — relations"},{"q":"The pituitary gland is related on either side to which structure?","choices":["Sphenoid air sinus","Cavernous sinus","Optic chiasma","Third ventricle"],"answer":1,"exp":"The gland is related on either side to the cavernous sinus. — 'Pituitary Gland (Hypophysis Cerebri)' — relations"},{"q":"Which subdivisions make up the anterior lobe (adenohypophysis) of the pituitary?","choices":["Pars nervosa, infundibulum, median eminence","Pars nervosa, pars distalis, pars tuberalis","Pars distalis, pars tuberalis, pars intermedia","Pars intermedia, pars nervosa, infundibular stalk"],"answer":2,"exp":"The adenohypophysis consists of the pars distalis, pars tuberalis, and pars intermedia. — 'The pituitary gland consists of' — anterior lobe subdivisions"},{"q":"The posterior lobe (neurohypophysis) consists of the pars nervosa, which is connected by the infundibulum to which part of the hypothalamus?","choices":["Optic chiasm","Mammillary body","Paraventricular nucleus","Median eminence (tuber cinereum)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pars nervosa connects via the infundibulum (pituitary stalk) to the median eminence (tuber cinereum) of the hypothalamus. — 'The pituitary gland consists of' — posterior lobe"},{"q":"Within the hypophyseal portal system, regulatory hormones leave the hypothalamus and pass through portal vessels to which target?","choices":["Adenohypophysis","Neurohypophysis","Suprarenal medulla","Pineal gland"],"answer":0,"exp":"Regulatory hormones travel through the fenestrated portal capillaries to the adenohypophysis (anterior pituitary). — 'The Hypophyseal Portal System'; 'Note' slide"},{"q":"The lecture's 'Note' slide contrasts the blood supply of the two pituitary lobes. Which statement is correct?","choices":["Both lobes receive portal blood","The anterior pituitary receives portal blood; the posterior receives arterial blood","The anterior pituitary receives arterial blood; the posterior receives portal blood","Both lobes receive only arterial blood"],"answer":1,"exp":"The anterior pituitary receives portal blood, while the posterior pituitary receives arterial blood. — 'Note' — anterior vs posterior pituitary blood supply"},{"q":"Somatotropes of the adenohypophysis release which hormone?","choices":["Prolactin (PRL)","Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH)","Growth hormone (GH)","Luteinizing hormone (LH)"],"answer":2,"exp":"Somatotropes release growth hormone (GH, also called somatotropin). — 'The Adenohypophysis' — five cell types"},{"q":"In the pars distalis, the acidophils (about 10% of cells, reddish cytoplasm) secrete which hormones?","choices":["TSH and ACTH","ADH and oxytocin","FSH and LH","Growth hormone and prolactin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Acidophils have reddish cytoplasm and secrete growth hormone (GH) and prolactin (lactogenic hormone). — 'Pars distalis is formed of' — acidophils vs basophils"},{"q":"The pars nervosa contains bundles of nerve fibers, pituicytes, and homogenous intercellular material that on fixation precipitates to form what?","choices":["Herring's body","Colloid","Chromaffin granules","Zymogen granules"],"answer":0,"exp":"On fixation the homogenous intercellular substance of the pars nervosa precipitates to form Herring's bodies. — 'Pars nervosa contains' — Herring's body"},{"q":"The thyroid gland lies in the lower neck spanning which vertebral levels?","choices":["C3 to C5","C5 to T1","T1 to T4","C1 to C3"],"answer":1,"exp":"The thyroid lies in the lower neck at the level of the 5th cervical (C5) to the 1st thoracic (T1) vertebrae. — 'THE THYROID GLAND' — vertebral levels"},{"q":"The apex of each thyroid lobe reaches to which laryngeal landmark?","choices":["Cricoid cartilage","Hyoid bone","Oblique line of the thyroid cartilage","4th or 5th tracheal ring"],"answer":2,"exp":"The apex is at the level of the oblique line of the thyroid cartilage of the larynx. — 'THE THYROID GLAND' — apex and base"},{"q":"The base of each thyroid lobe lies at the level of which structure?","choices":["Oblique line of thyroid cartilage","2nd and 3rd tracheal rings","1st tracheal ring","4th or 5th tracheal ring"],"answer":3,"exp":"The base of each lobe is at the level of the 4th or 5th tracheal ring. — 'THE THYROID GLAND' — apex and base"},{"q":"The anterior border of the thyroid lobe is thin and related to which vessel?","choices":["Anterior branch of the superior thyroid artery","Inferior thyroid artery","Thyroidea ima artery","Middle thyroid vein"],"answer":0,"exp":"The thin anterior border is related to the anterior branch of the superior thyroid artery and is continuous below with the upper border of the isthmus. — Thyroid borders — anterior border"},{"q":"The rounded posterior border of the thyroid is related below to the inferior thyroid artery and is the border to which the ______ glands are usually related.","choices":["Submandibular","Parathyroid","Thymus","Pineal"],"answer":1,"exp":"The parathyroid glands are usually related to the rounded posterior border of the thyroid. — Thyroid borders — posterior border"},{"q":"The medial surface of the thyroid is related to the larynx, pharynx, and which nerve, as well as the trachea, esophagus, and recurrent laryngeal nerve?","choices":["Vagus nerve","Phrenic nerve","External laryngeal nerve","Hypoglossal nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"The medial surface is related to the larynx, pharynx, and external laryngeal nerve (and to the trachea, esophagus, and recurrent laryngeal nerve). — 'The medial surface is related to'"},{"q":"The posterior surface of the thyroid is related to which structure overlapping the common carotid artery?","choices":["Sternomastoid muscle","Pretracheal fascia","Platysma","Carotid sheath"],"answer":3,"exp":"The posterior surface is related to the carotid sheath, which overlaps the common carotid artery. — 'The posterior surface is related to' — carotid sheath"},{"q":"The thyroid gland moves up and down with deglutition because it is enclosed in which fascia, connected above to the thyroid cartilage?","choices":["Pretracheal fascia","Prevertebral fascia","Carotid sheath","Superficial fascia"],"answer":0,"exp":"The gland lies within the pretracheal fascia, which is connected above to the thyroid cartilage — the reason it moves with swallowing. — Thyroid surfaces — pretracheal fascia / deglutition"},{"q":"The thyroid isthmus lies opposite which tracheal rings?","choices":["1st and 2nd","2nd and 3rd","4th and 5th","3rd and 4th"],"answer":1,"exp":"The isthmus lies opposite the 2nd and 3rd tracheal rings and is covered by the sternohyoid and sternothyroid muscles. — 'Thyroid isthmus'"},{"q":"The superior thyroid artery arises from which vessel?","choices":["Arch of the aorta","Thyrocervical trunk","External carotid artery","Internal carotid artery"],"answer":2,"exp":"The superior thyroid artery arises from the external carotid artery and descends with the external laryngeal nerve to the gland. — 'Blood supply of thyroid gland' — superior thyroid artery"},{"q":"The inferior thyroid artery arises from the thyrocervical trunk and becomes related to which nerve at the posterior surface of the gland?","choices":["External laryngeal nerve","Phrenic nerve","Vagus nerve","Recurrent laryngeal nerve"],"answer":3,"exp":"The inferior thyroid artery becomes related to the recurrent laryngeal nerve at the posterior surface of the gland. — 'Blood supply of thyroid gland' — inferior thyroid artery"},{"q":"The thyroidea ima artery, an uncommon variant supplying the isthmus, arises from the arch of the aorta or which other vessel?","choices":["Brachiocephalic artery","Thyrocervical trunk","External carotid artery","Vertebral artery"],"answer":0,"exp":"The thyroidea ima artery arises from the arch of the aorta or the brachiocephalic artery. — 'Blood supply of thyroid gland' — thyroidea ima artery"},{"q":"Regarding venous drainage of the thyroid, the inferior thyroid vein ends in which vein?","choices":["Internal jugular vein","Brachiocephalic vein","External jugular vein","Subclavian vein"],"answer":1,"exp":"The inferior thyroid vein ends in the brachiocephalic vein, whereas the superior and middle thyroid veins usually end in the internal jugular vein. — 'Venous drainage' — thyroid veins"},{"q":"The parafollicular (C) cells of the thyroid sit near the follicular basement lamina, separated from the follicle lumen by follicular epithelium, and secrete which hormone?","choices":["Thyroxine","Parathyroid hormone","Calcitonin","Thyroglobulin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Parafollicular (C) cells secrete calcitonin; they are larger and lighter-staining than follicular cells. — 'Para follicular or C cells' — calcitonin"},{"q":"The parathyroid glands are usually located in which position relative to the thyroid?","choices":["Anterior surface of the thyroid","Superior to the hyoid bone","Within the isthmus only","Posterior aspect of the thyroid"],"answer":3,"exp":"The parathyroid glands lie on the posterior aspect of the thyroid, between its posterior borders and capsule; sometimes tissue is embedded within the thyroid. — 'Parathyroid Glands (Post. view of thyroid)'; 'PARATHYROID GLANDS'"},{"q":"How many parathyroid glands are usually present, and how are they arranged?","choices":["Four total, two on each side (one superior, one inferior)","Two total, one on each side","Six total, three on each side","Three total, all on the left"],"answer":0,"exp":"There are usually two on each side — one superior and one inferior — for four total. — 'PARATHYROID GLANDS' — number and arrangement"},{"q":"Which parathyroid glands are more constant in location, usually lying midway along the posterior thyroid borders?","choices":["The inferior glands","The superior glands","The left-sided glands","The embedded glands"],"answer":1,"exp":"The superior parathyroid glands are more constant in location than the inferior and lie midway along the posterior thyroid borders. — 'PARATHYROID GLANDS' — superior vs inferior constancy"},{"q":"The inferior pair of parathyroid glands may be found in which of the following positions?","choices":["Anterior to the sternohyoid muscle","Within the carotid sheath","Above or below the inferior thyroid arteries, or in the thyroid near its base","Deep to the trachea in the superior mediastinum only"],"answer":2,"exp":"The inferior pair may lie above or below the inferior thyroid arteries, or within the thyroid gland near its base. — 'PARATHYROID GLANDS' — 'The inferior pair may be'"},{"q":"In parathyroid histology, which cells have slightly eosinophilic cytoplasm and produce parathyroid hormone (PTH)?","choices":["Oxyphil cells","Follicular cells","Chromaffin cells","Principal (chief) cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Principal (chief) cells have slightly eosinophilic cytoplasm and produce parathyroid hormone (PTH). — 'Histology of parathyroid glands' — principal (chief) cells"},{"q":"The oxyphil cells of the parathyroid gland are described as:","choices":["Larger and very eosinophilic cells of unknown function","Small cells with no granules","Simple cuboidal cells surrounding colloid","Lipid-droplet-laden cells in radial columns"],"answer":0,"exp":"Oxyphil cells are larger and very eosinophilic; their function is unknown, though they may also secrete PTH. — 'Histology of parathyroid glands' — oxyphil cells"},{"q":"The large rounded head of the pancreas fills the concavity of which structure?","choices":["Spleen","Duodenum","Stomach","Transverse colon"],"answer":1,"exp":"The head of the pancreas fills the concavity of the duodenum, while the pointed tail extends toward the spleen. — 'The Pancreas and Other Endocrine Tissues'; 'Head fills concavity of duodenum'"},{"q":"The tail of the pancreas extends toward which organ?","choices":["Liver","Left kidney","Spleen","Gallbladder"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pointed tail of the pancreas extends toward the spleen (reaching the hilus of the spleen). — Pancreas gross anatomy — 'Tail reaches hilus of the spleen'"},{"q":"The pancreas lies retroperitoneally at approximately which vertebral levels?","choices":["T6–T8","T9–T10","L4–L5","T12/L1 to L3"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pancreas lies retroperitoneally at approximately T12/L1 to L3. — Pancreas — 'Lies retroperitoneally at ~T-12/L-1 to L-3'"},{"q":"Within a pancreatic islet, which endocrine cell type produces insulin?","choices":["Beta (β) cells","Alpha (α) cells","Delta (δ) cells","F cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Beta (β) cells produce insulin. — Figure 19.10ab — endocrine cells of the pancreatic islet"},{"q":"Per the islet cell labeling, which cells produce glucagon?","choices":["Beta (β) cells","Alpha (α) cells","Delta (δ) cells","F cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Alpha (α) cells produce glucagon. — Figure 19.10ab — α cells (glucagon)"},{"q":"The main pancreatic duct (Duct of Wirsung) joins the common bile duct at which site?","choices":["Minor duodenal papilla","Pylorus","Ampulla of Vater","Neck of the pancreas"],"answer":2,"exp":"The Duct of Wirsung joins the CBD at the Ampulla of Vater (about 3–4\" below the pylorus). — 'Pancreatic Duct System' — Duct of Wirsung"},{"q":"The accessory pancreatic duct (Duct of Santorini) drains into the duodenum at which site?","choices":["Major duodenal papilla","Duodenojejunal flexure","Ampulla of Vater","Minor duodenal papilla"],"answer":3,"exp":"The Duct of Santorini joins the duodenum at the minor papilla and represents part of the duct from the dorsal pancreas. — 'Duct of Santorini' — accessory pancreatic duct"},{"q":"The suprarenal glands are located attached to which border of the kidneys?","choices":["Superior border","Inferior border","Medial border only","Lateral border only"],"answer":0,"exp":"The suprarenal (adrenal) glands are attached to the superior border of the kidneys. — 'The Suprarenal Glands' — location"},{"q":"The suprarenal glands receive a highly vascular supply. Which of the following is one of the arteries named in the lecture?","choices":["Splenic artery","Middle suprarenal artery from the descending aorta","Gastroduodenal artery","Superior thyroid artery"],"answer":1,"exp":"The middle suprarenal artery from the descending aorta supplies the gland, along with branches from the renal artery and the inferior phrenic artery. — 'The Suprarenal Glands' — arterial supply"},{"q":"The three distinct zones of the suprarenal cortex, from outer to inner, are:","choices":["Zona reticularis, fasciculata, glomerulosa","Zona fasciculata, glomerulosa, reticularis","Zona glomerulosa, fasciculata, reticularis","Medulla, cortex, capsule"],"answer":2,"exp":"From outer to inner the cortex is zona glomerulosa, zona fasciculata, and zona reticularis (deep to the capsule, superficial to the medulla). — 'The Suprarenal Glands' — cortical zones; Figure (c/d)"},{"q":"The zona glomerulosa of the adrenal cortex secretes which class of hormone?","choices":["Catecholamines","Glucocorticoids (cortisol)","Androgens","Mineralocorticoids (aldosterone)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The zona glomerulosa secretes mineralocorticoids (aldosterone). — 'HISTOLOGY OF ADRENAL GLANDS' — zona glomerulosa"},{"q":"The zona fasciculata, composed of lipid-droplet-laden cells in radial columns, secretes which hormone?","choices":["Glucocorticoids (cortisol)","Aldosterone","Androgens","Adrenaline"],"answer":0,"exp":"The zona fasciculata secretes glucocorticoids (cortisol). — 'HISTOLOGY OF ADRENAL GLANDS' — zona fasciculata"},{"q":"The principal cells of the adrenal medulla, considered modified postganglionic sympathetic neurons lacking dendrites and axons, are called:","choices":["Chief cells","Chromaffin cells","Pituicytes","Oxyphil cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"The chromaffin cells are the principal cells of the medulla; they secrete adrenaline and noradrenaline, and the medulla contains a large central vein. — 'ADRENAL MEDULLA' — chromaffin cells"}]};
